## 📂 Suggested Folder Structure

```
ai-image-storyteller/
│── package.json
│── .env
│── server.js              # main entry (keeps very slim)
│
├── src/                   # all backend code lives here
│   ├── routes/            # API routes
│   │   └── story.js
│   ├── utils/             # helper functions (image→base64, etc.)
│   │   └── fileToBase64.js
│   └── config/            # config & setup
│       └── openai.js
│
├── public/                # frontend (static)
│   ├── index.html
│   └── assets/            # (later: CSS, images, icons)
│
└── README.md              # docs
```

---

### 🔹 Why this way?  
- **`src/routes/`** → keeps each route small & focused (`story.js`, later you could add `/health.js`, etc.).  
- **`src/utils/`** → where you dump helpers (like base64 conversion, logging).  
- **`src/config/`** → keeps external config clean (`openai.js` will load the client with your API key).  
- **`public/`** → totally separate frontend, easy to serve or swap out with React later.  
- **`server.js`** → becomes just the bootstrapping file, not a giant mess.  

This mirrors how real-world devs structure small Node projects: **routes, utils, config, public**.  

